architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "0002"
  title: Use pest as the Rust parsing engine
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-07-11"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: [[[
    syon-parser went through three parsing approaches before settling.
    First, winnow: a hand-written combinator parser over the lexer's
    token stream (the initial scaffold). Second, saphyr-parser 0.0.6, a
    YAML 1.2 event parser, on the theory that SYON's Block 1 is a YAML
    block-style subset, so an existing YAML event stream could be
    filtered to reject the forbidden construct set -- this is still the
    strategy an earlier revision of spec/02-grammar.md described. It
    required a separate preflight text scan alongside it, because
    saphyr-parser's event stream doesn't carry block-vs-flow style
    information: the events for a flow sequence and a hypothetical
    block-style equivalent look the same, so flow collections couldn't
    be rejected from the event stream alone. Third, and current: pest, a
    native PEG grammar (crates/syon-parser/src/grammar.pest) encoding
    SYON's structure and spacing rule directly, with an
    indentation-aware builder walking the flat pest output into the
    typed AST.
    ]]]
  decision: [[[
    Use pest as the parsing engine, with SYON's grammar defined natively
    in grammar.pest rather than adapting a general-purpose YAML parser.
    Reusing a YAML parser seemed attractive since Block 1 is a YAML
    subset, but SYON's spacing rule (: / - / # are structural only when
    followed by whitespace) and its non-YAML constructs (Block 2 fences,
    Block 3 literal blocks) don't map onto YAML's event model cleanly. A
    native PEG grammar can express all three block types and the spacing
    rule directly, without fighting an external parser's assumptions.
    ]]]
  consequences:
    positive:
      - The grammar is fully owned and auditable in one file (grammar.pest) rather than depending on a YAML parser's interpretation of flow versus block style.
    negative:
      - spec/02-grammar.md's earlier note recommending saphyr-parser described the rejected second approach, not the current implementation, and needed correcting.
      - The preflight text-scan pattern introduced to work around saphyr-parser's lack of style information was kept after switching to pest rather than folded entirely into the grammar -- see ADR 0003 for the real bug that caused.
      - syon-go (ADR 0004) is an independent implementation and does not use pest or any parsing library -- it has its own hand-written recursive-descent parser, so this decision only binds the Rust implementation.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: winnow (hand-written combinator parser)
      reason: Initial scaffold approach; superseded before the spec's three-block model and spacing rule stabilized.
    -
      name: saphyr-parser (YAML 1.2 event parser)
      reason: Event stream carries no block-vs-flow style information, so flow collections couldn't be rejected without a bolted-on text scan; SYON-only constructs (fences, literal blocks) don't map onto YAML events at all.
